    "title": "Chicago Teachers Union Leader Takes Helm of Statewide Union - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Laura Mitchell ,\n3 hours ago President of the Chicago Teachers Union Stacy Davis Gates has been elected president of the Illinois Federation of Teachers while continuing her role at CTU. Critics have pushed back on the appointment, citing low test scores and her previous opposition to standardized testing. Gates plans to advocate for increased school funding and reforms to the state tax code.\nFormer Chicago schools superintendent Paul Vallas said, “They’re a political party. They don’t give a (expletive) about the kids.”\nVallas added, “The Socialist Party of Chicago is the Chicago’s Teachers Union.”\nGates previously negotiated two contracts for over 25,000 Chicago teachers, approved by about 97% of CTU members. Her CTU salary is reported at $195,000.\nGates said, “The belly of a child who just had their one hot meal a day cut from school budgets rumbles the same, whether that young person is on the southside of Chicago or in Cahokia.”\nDan Montgomery left the IFT to lead the American Library Association, with Gates succeeding him as president. The IFT is planning a fall lobbying day to push its funding agenda.\nState lawmakers have paused $43 million of a planned $350 million school funding increase for review under the 2017 law.\nLaura Mitchell covers U.S. politics & news for content partner Modern Newsstand LLC.",
